RISC OS Open Release Archive
============================

This is the latest desktop filer, filing system, and low level driver which
works with solid state NonVolatile Memory express (NVMe) type drives.

The three components included in this archive are a joint Open Source
development between RISCOSbits, Stader Softwareentwicklung, and RISC OS Open.


Supported hardware
==================

Currently this software only supports the Raspberry Pi Compute Module 4. It
is anticipated that support for other platforms with a PCI express socket such
as the Titanium and Pinebook Pro will be added in due course.

A broad selection of drives are available with sizes up to 256GB and 2TB
possible with FileCore; contact your RISC OS dealer for advice on buying
specific models.


How to install the software
===========================

The add on is provided in the form of an update to !Boot which can be merged
using the Configure application.

1. Start Configure (see chapter "Changing the computer's configuration"
   in the RISC OS 5 User Guide)
2. Click on the Boot setup plug-in
3. Click on the Install option
4. Drag the !Boot update from this archive to the drop site
5. Click on the Merge button
6. Reboot the Raspberry Pi

When it restarts you will find a new fixed disc icon on the lefthand side
of the icon bar.

If you purchased the drive blank from a non-RISC OS supplier it may be
necessary to format the drive before use. For this, HForm 2.78 or later is
required to understand NVMe drives. You can get the latest version of HForm
via PackMan or by downloading it from
  https://packages.riscosopen.org/rool/DiscDetails.html
directly.


Are you into programming?
=========================

If you're into programming and would like to help out enhancing the software,
or are merely interested to see how it works, the source code is available
to download from the RISC OS Open GitLab from the following addresses:

  https://gitlab.riscosopen.org/RiscOS/Sources/FileSys/ADFS/ADFSFiler
  https://gitlab.riscosopen.org/RiscOS/Sources/FileSys/NVMeFS/NVMeFS
  https://gitlab.riscosopen.org/RiscOS/Sources/HWSupport/NVMe/NVMeDriver

Note: despite its name, the ADFSFiler component has assembly time selections
      to produce the NVMeFiler as an option.


Caution
=======

As a new piece of software, it is possible that there are bugs hiding that
nobody has discovered yet. As with any disc drive, it is important to have
a robust backup strategy in place should things go wrong.


